WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.632 --> 00:00:07.837
It has been said that "History is written by the victors."

2
00:00:07.838 --> 00:00:13.111
In other words, it's those who win battles, not those who lose them, who end up influencing history.

3
00:00:13.112 --> 00:00:21.993
William the Conqueror was definitely victorious at the Battle of Hastings in 1066, when he took control of Anglo-Saxon England.

4
00:00:21.994 --> 00:00:27.532
Although he lived a thousand years ago, his influence can still be felt today in many parts of the world.

5
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:33.807
"History is written by the victors" also means that the victor's version of events is usually the one that is remembered.

6
00:00:33.808 --> 00:00:43.480
However, the Normans' version of their conquest of England was not written down on paper; it was embroidered on a long piece of cloth known as the Bayeux Tapestry.

7
00:00:43.481 --> 00:00:49.702
Created about thirty years after the invasion, it features over seventy different scenes with Latin captions.

8
00:00:49.703 --> 00:00:57.400
In fact, it looks like a kind of medieval comic strip, and remains a crucial source of information about these long-ago events.

9
00:00:57.401 --> 00:01:03.789
So who was William? This Norman is considered to be one of the greatest soldiers and rulers of the Middle Ages.

10
00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:08.284
The word "Norman" comes from the word "Norsemen," meaning "people of the north."

11
00:01:08.285 --> 00:01:17.563
Excellent sailors and ferocious fighters, the Norsemen—also known as Vikings—came from Norway, Sweden, Denmark, and Iceland.

12
00:01:17.564 --> 00:01:24.063
In 913, some Norsemen invaded northern France and settled in an area that became known as Normandy.

13
00:01:24.064 --> 00:01:29.341
Although they pledged loyalty to the French king, they remained very independent and powerful.

14
00:01:29.342 --> 00:01:35.205
In 1035, William's father died, and the seven-year-old boy became duke of Normandy.

15
00:01:35.206 --> 00:01:40.830
But William was illegitimate, and as a result, many people did not want him to rule.

16
00:01:40.831 --> 00:01:46.769
Conflicts broke out, and three of William's guardians died violently—even his tutor was murdered.

17
00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:51.650
But William managed to survive, and these early challenges helped form his character.

18
00:01:51.651 --> 00:01:56.149
He was intelligent, strong, energetic, determined, and a fierce soldier.

19
00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:01.139
The Bayeux Tapestry's first scene, however, shows not William, but his cousin, Edward.

20
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:06.366
Edward's grandmother had married into the English ruling family, and Edward became king.

21
00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:14.069
He had no children, and according to the tapestry, had promised to make William his heir. But William faced serious competition.

22
00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:21.824
Two powerful Anglo-Saxon noblemen, Harold and his brother Tostig, wanted the throne; so did the king of Norway.

23
00:02:21.825 --> 00:02:29.177
Before Edward died in January 1066, he chose Harold as his successor, and Harold was crowned king.

24
00:02:29.178 --> 00:02:35.103
According to the tapestry, however, Harold had sworn an oath to support William's claim to the throne.

25
00:02:35.104 --> 00:02:39.056
William felt deeply betrayed and decided to invade England.

26
00:02:39.057 --> 00:02:47.071
William hoped to sail to England in August, 1066, but the winds were not good for crossing the English Channel, and his ships had to wait.

27
00:02:47.072 --> 00:02:50.864
In early September, the Normans were still waiting for favorable winds.

28
00:02:50.865 --> 00:02:57.533
Unfortunately for Harold, most of his southern army consisted of peasants who needed to return home to harvest their crops.

29
00:02:57.534 --> 00:03:00.683
So on September 8, he reluctantly let them go.

30
00:03:00.684 --> 00:03:03.140
But Harold's troubles were just beginning.

31
00:03:03.141 --> 00:03:09.845
Tostig and the king of Norway were now invading in the north of England, and he was forced to gather more troops to confront them.

32
00:03:09.846 --> 00:03:16.240
On September 25, Harold defeated both his opponents, and he and his men began to march back to the south.

33
00:03:16.241 --> 00:03:24.463
Back in France, the winds had finally changed, and on September 28, the Normans landed near Hastings, on England's southeast coast.

34
00:03:24.464 --> 00:03:32.098
About two weeks later, Harold finally reached Hastings, his foot soldiers exhausted from fighting and marching.

35
00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:40.044
William's troops were well rested, and his army included archers and about two thousand cavalry in addition to infantry.

36
00:03:40.045 --> 00:03:46.391
On the morning of October 14, before the English could properly prepare for battle, the Normans attacked.

37
00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:54.376
The English soldiers defended themselves by forming a shield wall; they lined up side by side in rows and locked their shields together.

38
00:03:54.377 --> 00:04:02.648
At first, the Normans could not break through this wall, and they suffered many casualties due to the Anglo-Saxon's two-handed battle axes.

39
00:04:02.649 --> 00:04:11.908
Soon, the Norman cavalry panicked and fled. Rumors flew that William had been killed, so William removed his helmet to show he was alive.

40
00:04:11.909 --> 00:04:20.364
Inspired, his men turned back to the battlefield and were able to destroy the English troops, who had broken formation to follow the retreating Normans.

41
00:04:20.365 --> 00:04:30.901
According to the Bayeux Tapestry, by nightfall, Harold was dead (the result of an arrow to his eye), his troops were in disarray, and the Normans had won the Battle of Hastings.

42
00:04:30.902 --> 00:04:38.614
William was crowned king of England on Christmas Day, 1066, and began taking land away from the English to give to Norman lords.

43
00:04:38.615 --> 00:04:42.286
Many Anglo-Saxons rebelled, especially in the north.

44
00:04:42.287 --> 00:04:49.827
The Normans slew them by the hundreds and destroyed farms and villages, even killing livestock so that any survivors starved to death.

45
00:04:49.828 --> 00:04:55.827
With the English finally subdued, William set about establishing a system of rule known as the feudal system.

46
00:04:55.828 --> 00:05:02.711
He granted land and power to certain lords, who swore loyalty to the king and agreed to supply him with soldiers in wartime.

47
00:05:02.712 --> 00:05:06.644
The lords, in return, leased parts of their lands to their knights.

48
00:05:06.645 --> 00:05:11.854
At the bottom of this social system were the peasants, who worked the land and served those above them.

49
00:05:11.855 --> 00:05:17.186
The Anglo-Saxons had never built castles, but the Norman nobles built them everywhere.

50
00:05:17.187 --> 00:05:26.251
From within these castles, they governed their subjects and organized armies, and the castles became very visible reminders of Norman power and the feudal system.

51
00:05:26.252 --> 00:05:35.021
Modern historians know much about the period immediately following the invasion from the Domesday Book, a survey of England conducted in 1086.

52
00:05:35.022 --> 00:05:43.967
Written on sheepskin parchment, the book describes 13,418 places, most of which, amazingly, can be found on maps today.

53
00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:51.007
For each place, the book records details about the people living there, the buildings, the land, the farm animals, and more.

54
00:05:51.008 --> 00:05:53.782
Even the numbers of wild horses are listed.

55
00:05:53.783 --> 00:06:02.722
Originally compiled so William knew how much each plot of land should be taxed, this remarkable book gives us a "snapshot" of England in the eleventh century.

56
00:06:02.723 --> 00:06:06.610
It remains one of England's most significant historical documents.

57
00:06:06.611 --> 00:06:10.327
William also made changes to the administration of justice in England.

58
00:06:10.328 --> 00:06:16.589
Before the Norman conquest, laws and customs could vary from village to village and from decade to decade.

59
00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:24.651
William centralized the legal system with a royal court, the King's Court, and sent out judges all over the land to uphold the country's laws.

60
00:06:24.652 --> 00:06:29.793
This system became known as "common law" because it applied to everyone in the same way.

61
00:06:29.794 --> 00:06:34.640
To this day, democracies base their judicial systems on common law.

62
00:06:34.641 --> 00:06:41.252
Last but not least, the English language as it is spoken today is a direct result of the Norman invasion.

63
00:06:41.253 --> 00:06:48.347
The Norman nobles spoke French and Latin, and while they did not replace Anglo-Saxon English, they certainly changed it.

64
00:06:48.348 --> 00:06:54.940
Under Norman influence, English became more flexible, acquiring thousands of new words from the Norman tongues.

65
00:06:54.941 --> 00:07:03.933
The Normans prove that history is literally written by the victors, since historians have only the documents left by the conquerors of the Anglo-Saxons.

66
00:07:03.934 --> 00:07:16.637
Many people argue that these accounts are not accurate and not fair to the Anglo-Saxons, but sadly, they may never know Harold's side of the story since he, unlike the victors, left no records behind.
